---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const pathname = Astro.url.pathname;
const toc = [
  { href: "#overview", label: "Overview" },
  { href: "#installation", label: "Installation" },
  { href: "#public-interfaces", label: "Public interfaces" },
  { href: "#documentation", label: "Documentation" },
  { href: "#project-model", label: "Project model" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Linkar documentation"
  description="Documentation for Linkar, a lightweight runtime for reusable analysis templates, Git-backed packs, project metadata, and automation interfaces."
>
  <div class="docs-page-shell">
    <DocsSidebar currentPath={pathname} />
    <main class="docs-shell docs-shell-with-toc">
      <article class="docs-content prose docs-home-content">
        <p class="docs-badges" aria-label="Project links">
          <a href="https://github.com/chaochungkuo/linkar" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/chaochungkuo/linkar/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Issues</a>
          <a href={`${base}docs/`}>Docs</a>
        </p>

        <h1 id="overview">Linkar documentation</h1>
        <p class="lede article-lede">
          Linkar is a lightweight runtime for reusable computational templates. It helps teams
          publish scripts, CLI tools, notebooks, and analysis steps as Git-backed packs with
          traceable local runs for humans, scripts, and agents.
        </p>

        <p>
          The core idea is deliberately small: packs distribute reusable templates and bindings,
          projects record local work, and every run leaves readable artifacts on disk. Linkar does
          not try to become a workflow engine. It provides the contract around shared analysis
          building blocks.
        </p>

        <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>
        <p>
          Install Linkar into the environment where you want to manage packs and project runs.
        </p>
        <pre><code>pip install linkar</code></pre>

        <p>
          After installation, add a pack and initialize a project:
        </p>
        <pre><code>linkar config pack add github:IZKF-Genomics/izkf_pack --id izkf_pack
linkar project init --name study
cd study</code></pre>

        <h2 id="public-interfaces">Public interfaces</h2>
        <p>
          Linkar exposes the same runtime semantics through three stable user-facing interfaces.
        </p>

        <div class="docs-reference-cards">
          <a href={`${base}explanations/interfaces-and-automation/`}>
            <strong>CLI</strong>
            <span>Run, render, collect, inspect, clean, and manage projects from the terminal.</span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}explanations/local-api-reference/`}>
            <strong>Local API</strong>
            <span>Resolve templates and read run metadata through local HTTP endpoints.</span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}tutorials/using-linkar-from-agents/`}>
            <strong>MCP</strong>
            <span>Expose Linkar operations to agent clients without shell scraping.</span>
          </a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="documentation">Documentation</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the guides if you are learning by doing. Use the conceptual pages when you
          need the runtime model, project ledger, cleanup contract, or automation boundary.
        </p>

        <div class="docs-reference-cards">
          <a href={`${base}tutorials/getting-started/`}>
            <strong>Installation and first run</strong>
            <span>Create a project, run a template, and inspect outputs.</span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}explanations/template-runtime-contract/`}>
            <strong>Template contract</strong>
            <span>Understand params, outputs, cleanup rules, tools, and run modes.</span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}examples/`}>
            <strong>Examples</strong>
            <span>Choose a bundled example pack based on the pattern you want to inspect.</span>
          </a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="project-model">Project model</h2>
        <p>
          A Linkar project is a local directory with a project ledger, stable visible run paths,
          and optional immutable run history. This keeps analysis handoffs readable while still
          retaining enough metadata for provenance.
        </p>
        <pre><code>linkar render demultiplex --outdir ./demultiplex
bash ./demultiplex/run.sh
linkar collect ./demultiplex
linkar clean .</code></pre>
      </article>

      <aside class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
        <nav class="toc-nav">
          {toc.map((item) => (
            <a class="toc-link" href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      </aside>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
